{"name":"Raffia White","hex":"#eeeee3","fetched":"2026-10-12","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"raffia","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["A fibrous material used for tying plants, originating from the leaves of raffia palm trees (genus Raphia)."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/raffia"},{"term":"white","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Adjective","definitions":["Bright and colourless; reflecting equal quantities of all frequencies of visible light.","Of or relating to Europeans or those of European descent, regardless if their skin has cool or warm undertones."]},{"part":"Noun","definitions":["The color of snow or milk; the color of light containing equal amounts of all visible wavelengths.","A person of European descent with light-colored skin."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/white"}],"entries":[{"title":"Raffia palm","blocks":[{"text":"Raffia palms are members of the genus Raphia. The Malagasy name rafia is derived from fia \"to squeeze juice\". The genus contains about twenty species of palms native to tropical regions of Africa, and especially Madagascar, with one species also occurring in Central and South America. R. taedigera is the source of raffia fibers, which are the veins of the leaves, and this species produces a fruit called \"brazilia pods\", \"uxi nuts\" or \"uxi pods\"."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/7/71/Raphia_australis.jpg/330px-Raphia_australis.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Raffia_palm"},{"title":"White","blocks":[{"text":"White is the lightest color and is achromatic. It is the color of objects such as snow, chalk, and milk, and is the opposite of black. White objects fully reflect and scatter all the visible wavelengths of light. White on television and computer screens is created by a mixture of red, blue, and green light. The color white can be given with white pigments, especially titanium dioxide."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/e/e2/Delphinapterus_leucas_2.jpg/330px-Delphinapterus_leucas_2.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/White"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/f/ff/Making_raffia_rope.jpg/500px-Making_raffia_rope.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/f/ff/Making_raffia_rope.jpg/500px-Making_raffia_rope.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Removing the membrane","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AMaking_raffia_rope.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/0/0e/Rolling_raffia_rope.jpg/500px-Rolling_raffia_rope.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/0/0e/Rolling_raffia_rope.jpg/500px-Rolling_raffia_rope.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Rolling the fibres together before drying","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3ARolling_raffia_rope.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/e/ed/Raphia_weaver_in_Babouantou.jpg/500px-Raphia_weaver_in_Babouantou.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/e/ed/Raphia_weaver_in_Babouantou.jpg/1280px-Raphia_weaver_in_Babouantou.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/e/ed/Raphia_weaver_in_Babouantou.jpg/500px-Raphia_weaver_in_Babouantou.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Weaving raffia","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3ARaphia_weaver_in_Babouantou.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wiki